{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"id\":\"R-1:1\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The movement from بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ to ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ shifts from naming God in invocation to declaring praise as due to Him; رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ then expands the name into a relation with all worlds.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:1\",\"ar\":\"بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The same name Allah closes the first phrase and governs the praise phrase that follows.\",\"limits\":\"The sequence establishes a change in utterance and expansion of titles; it does not state that invocation causes praise.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-1:3\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The repeated ٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ after رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ places mercy beside universal lordship, so the next hearing of the Lord is explicitly merciful.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:3\",\"ar\":\"ٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ\"}],\"activation\":\"The titles are adjacent in the Fatiha, and the mercy pair already appeared with Allah in 1:1.\",\"limits\":\"The sequence juxtaposes lordship and mercy without defining one as the cause or full content of the other.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-1:4\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The title مَٰلِكِ يَوْمِ ٱلدِّينِ carries the praise of رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ into final judgment: the praised Lord of all worlds is also named in relation to the day of recompense.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:4\",\"ar\":\"مَٰلِكِ يَوْمِ ٱلدِّينِ\"}],\"activation\":\"The second title follows immediately after the mercy pair and continues the opening description of God.\",\"limits\":\"The wording does not equate all worlds with the day of judgment; it adds that day as a further domain of divine rule.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-1:5\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"After praise and divine titles in the third person, إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ turns to direct address and names worship and seeking help as the speakers’ response to the one just praised.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:5\",\"ar\":\"إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ وَإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ\"}],\"activation\":\"The passage moves from describing Allah to addressing Him with fronted إِيَّاكَ and first-person plural verbs.\",\"limits\":\"The sequence supports a rhetorical relation between praise and worship, but does not state an explicit causal particle.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-1:6\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"The request ٱهْدِنَا follows the declaration of worship and reliance, making guidance a concrete need within the relation to the Lord praised in 1:2.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:5\",\"ar\":\"وَإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The same first-person plural speakers move directly from asking for help to asking for guidance.\",\"limits\":\"The text does not explicitly call guidance an act of lordship in 1:2.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-1:7\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"The path request is specified by a community marked by favor and by two contrary outcomes; this gives a moral and communal contour to the guidance sought after praising the Lord of all worlds.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:7\",\"ar\":\"صِرَٰطَ ٱلَّذِينَ أَنْعَمْتَ عَلَيْهِمْ غَيْرِ ٱلْمَغْضُوبِ عَلَيْهِمْ وَلَا ٱلضَّآلِّينَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The Fatiha moves from universal lordship to a request whose path is defined by human groups and outcomes.\",\"limits\":\"The words do not explicitly identify the groups in 1:7 with all the worlds named in 1:2.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-2:5\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The phrase هُدًى مِّن رَّبِّهِم gives a Quranic instance of guidance coming from the Lord; in the Fatiha, the Lord of all worlds is followed by the request ٱهْدِنَا.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"},{\"ref\":\"2:5\",\"ar\":\"أُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ عَلَىٰ هُدًۭى مِّن رَّبِّهِمْ\"}],\"activation\":\"The shared pairing of guidance and Rabb connects the Fatiha’s petition with the description in 2:5.\",\"limits\":\"The verses do not identify the addressed groups in 2:5 with the Fatiha’s speakers.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-2:98\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-2:131\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Ibrahim’s أَسْلَمْتُ لِرَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ turns the same universal Lord title into a personal declaration of submission.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"2:131\",\"ar\":\"قَالَ أَسْلَمْتُ لِرَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The title is repeated exactly, but in 2:131 it is the addressee of an individual’s answer to a command to submit.\",\"limits\":\"The verse supplies Ibrahim’s response, not a claim that every community responds this way.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-2:139\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"وَهُوَ رَبُّنَا وَرَبُّكُمْ uses shared lordship to reject a boundary between the disputing communities; this gives رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ a direct implication against restricting God to one party.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"2:139\",\"ar\":\"وَهُوَ رَبُّنَا وَرَبُّكُمْ\"}],\"activation\":\"The plural possessives explicitly make God both our Lord and your Lord in a dispute about God.\",\"limits\":\"This verse does not use the full title ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ or state that all parties worship rightly.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-3:51\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"عِيسَىٰ’s إنَّ ٱللَّهَ رَبِّى وَرَبُّكُمْ joins shared lordship to the command فَٱعْبُدُوهُ and the straight path; it parallels the Fatiha’s movement from Rabb al-alamin to worship and guidance.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:5\",\"ar\":\"إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"},{\"ref\":\"3:51\",\"ar\":\"إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ رَبِّى وَرَبُّكُمْ فَٱعْبُدُوهُ ۗ هَٰذَا صِرَٰطٌۭ مُّسْتَقِيمٌۭ\"}],\"activation\":\"The two passages connect Rabb, worship, and a straight path in the same sequence of ideas.\",\"limits\":\"The wording does not establish that 3:51 quotes the Fatiha.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-3:63\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-3:79\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"رَبَّٰنِيِّۦنَ shares the ر ب ب root visible in رَبِّ, which may connect the Lord of all worlds with a human formation oriented to scripture and its teaching.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"3:79\",\"ar\":\"وَلَٰكِن كُونُوا۟ رَبَّٰنِيِّۦنَ بِمَا كُنتُمْ تُعَلِّمُونَ ٱلْكِتَٰبَ وَبِمَا كُنتُمْ تَدْرُسُونَ\"}],\"missing\":\"A dictionary account of رَبَّانِيّ and its relation to رَبّ is needed to establish whether this is a meaningful lexical bridge or only a shared root; that sense could clarify what the human designation adds here.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-3:188\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The people who love to يُحْمَدُوا۟ for deeds they did not do contrast with ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ: the shared ح م د root places divine praise beside a warning about seeking undeserved human commendation.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ\"},{\"ref\":\"3:188\",\"ar\":\"وَيُحِبُّونَ أَن يُحْمَدُوا۟ بِمَا لَمْ يَفْعَلُوا۟\"}],\"activation\":\"The explicit praise root recurs in a passage about wanting praise without grounds.\",\"limits\":\"The contrast does not say that every human commendation is false or that the grammar of 1:2 excludes all other objects of praise.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-4:131\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The closing وَكَانَ ٱللَّهُ غَنِيًّا حَمِيدًۭا describes God as rich and worthy of praise even in a passage that allows for human disbelief; it frames the hamd of 1:2 as not dependent on human contribution.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ\"},{\"ref\":\"4:131\",\"ar\":\"وَإِن تَكْفُرُوا۟ فَإِنَّ لِلَّهِ مَا فِى ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ وَمَا فِى ٱلْأَرْضِ ۚ وَكَانَ ٱللَّهُ غَنِيًّا حَمِيدًۭا\"}],\"activation\":\"The same passage states that the heavens and earth belong to Allah and then names Him حَمِيدًا despite the disbelieving condition.\",\"limits\":\"حَمِيدٌ is a divine attribute, not the same grammatical form as ٱلْحَمْدُ; the relation is lexical and thematic.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-5:28\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The exact title رَبَّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ is spoken as the ground for refusing to kill: universal lordship appears here as an authority that restrains a person even when he faces a threat.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"5:28\",\"ar\":\"إِنِّىٓ أَخَافُ ٱللَّهَ رَبَّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"}],\"activation\":\"إِنِّىٓ أَخَافُ directly links fear of Allah to the same title used in the Fatiha.\",\"limits\":\"The verse does not say that praise itself is the reason for the speaker’s restraint.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-6:1\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The exact opening ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ is followed by creation of the heavens and earth, then by condemnation of those who equate others with their Lord; this gives the Fatiha’s praise a creation and anti-association setting.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"6:1\",\"ar\":\"ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ ٱلَّذِى خَلَقَ ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ وَٱلْأَرْضَ\"},{\"ref\":\"6:1\",\"ar\":\"ثُمَّ ٱلَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا۟ بِرَبِّهِمْ يَعْدِلُونَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The opening praise formula and the root ر ب ب recur alongside creation and the act of equating others with God.\",\"limits\":\"The focus does not itself state the creation acts or the condemnation expressed in 6:1.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-6:45\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"After the destruction of the unjust people, the narrative ends with the exact formula وَٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ; the Fatiha’s opening praise also serves in the Quran as a verdict after judgment has taken effect.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"6:45\",\"ar\":\"فَقُطِعَ دَابِرُ ٱلْقَوْمِ ٱلَّذِينَ ظَلَمُوا۟ ۚ وَٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The identical formula follows immediately after the people who acted unjustly are cut off.\",\"limits\":\"The Fatiha gives no such narrative event; 6:45 shows one consequential setting for the formula, not its only meaning.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-6:71\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The command to submit لِرَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ follows a warning against turning back after guidance; the Fatiha’s Rabb title likewise precedes its worship declaration and request for guidance.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:5\",\"ar\":\"إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"},{\"ref\":\"6:71\",\"ar\":\"وَأُمِرْنَا لِنُسْلِمَ لِرَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The same title is linked to submission in a passage whose preceding clause warns against reversal after guidance.\",\"limits\":\"The parallel does not prove that the speakers or circumstances of the two passages are identical.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-6:162\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The exact title رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ governs a declaration that prayer, sacrifice, life, and death belong to Allah; it extends the Fatiha’s praise and lordship frame across a person’s whole existence.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"6:162\",\"ar\":\"إِنَّ صَلَاتِى وَنُسُكِى وَمَحْيَاىَ وَمَمَاتِى لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The shared title follows a list spanning worship, living, and dying, with لِلَّهِ expressing their dedication.\",\"limits\":\"The verse describes a particular declaration of devotion and does not spell out every implication of the Fatiha’s praise clause.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-7:43\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The people of the Garden say ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ after acknowledging that Allah guided them there; this supplies an outcome to the Fatiha’s praise followed by a request for guidance.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"},{\"ref\":\"7:43\",\"ar\":\"وَقَالُوا۟ ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ ٱلَّذِى هَدَىٰنَا لِهَٰذَا\"}],\"activation\":\"The same praise formula is joined explicitly to completed guidance in 7:43.\",\"limits\":\"The two passages do not identify the Fatiha’s requested path with the Garden in this verse.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-7:61\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Noah and Hud both answer accusations about themselves by identifying their source as رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ; the Fatiha’s title thus also appears as a shared prophetic authority formula in disputes with communities.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"7:61\",\"ar\":\"وَلَٰكِنِّى رَسُولٌۭ مِّن رَّبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"7:67\",\"ar\":\"وَلَٰكِنِّى رَسُولٌۭ مِّن رَّبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The exact messenger formula recurs across two prophetic replies following accusations from their peoples.\",\"limits\":\"These verses establish the formula’s use in prophetic speech but do not define the full scope of ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-7:67\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"same-as:R-7:61\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-7:80\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"مِّنَ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ occurs in a claim about whether anyone among the worlds or peoples had done this before Lot’s community; this Quranic use supports hearing ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ in 1:2 as capable of including human communities.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"7:80\",\"ar\":\"مَا سَبَقَكُم بِهَا مِنْ أَحَدٍۢ مِّنَ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The same plural noun appears with مِنْ أَحَدٍ in a context referring to people or communities.\",\"limits\":\"This occurrence does not settle whether the Fatiha’s term refers to human communities alone or to a wider set of worlds.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-7:104\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Moses names رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ as the source of his message to Pharaoh, placing the universal title against a ruler who is being addressed directly.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"7:104\",\"ar\":\"إِنِّى رَسُولٌۭ مِّن رَّبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"7:105\",\"ar\":\"قَدْ جِئْتُكُم بِبَيِّنَةٍۢ مِّن رَّبِّكُمْ\"}],\"activation\":\"The next verse speaks of a clear proof from Pharaoh’s Lord, bringing universal and immediate address into the same confrontation.\",\"limits\":\"The passage does not state that Pharaoh accepts Moses’ claim or that the title is a direct quotation from the Fatiha.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-7:122\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The magicians’ رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ is immediately specified as رَبِّ مُوسَىٰ وَهَٰرُونَ; the universal title is identified in this scene through named prophetic messengers.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"7:121\",\"ar\":\"قَالُوٓا۟ ءَامَنَّا بِرَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"7:122\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ مُوسَىٰ وَهَٰرُونَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The successive clauses identify the Lord of the worlds with the Lord of Moses and Aaron.\",\"limits\":\"The scene provides this identification in the magicians’ confession; it does not reduce the title’s scope to these two prophets.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-7:125\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"Under Pharaoh’s threat, the believers answer that they return to رَبِّنَا; the passage gives Rabb an ultimate-destination force that can sit beside the Fatiha’s later title مَٰلِكِ يَوْمِ ٱلدِّينِ.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:4\",\"ar\":\"مَٰلِكِ يَوْمِ ٱلدِّينِ\"},{\"ref\":\"7:124\",\"ar\":\"ثُمَّ لَأُصَلِّبَنَّكُمْ أَجْمَعِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"7:125\",\"ar\":\"قَالُوٓا۟ إِنَّآ إِلَىٰ رَبِّنَا مُنقَلِبُونَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The return to their Lord is the believers’ answer to a death threat.\",\"limits\":\"This verse does not use ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ or explicitly name the day of judgment.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-8:53\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-9:27\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-9:31\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The plural أَرْبَابًا names human authorities taken as lords besides Allah; beside رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ, this exposes the rival lordship claim against which the Fatiha’s God-directed title can be heard.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"9:31\",\"ar\":\"ٱتَّخَذُوٓا۟ أَحْبَارَهُمْ وَرُهْبَٰنَهُمْ أَرْبَابًۭا مِّن دُونِ ٱللَّهِ\"}],\"activation\":\"The shared ر ب ب root occurs in an explicit contrast between Allah and others treated as lords.\",\"limits\":\"The verse does not say that every form of human authority is lordship or that the Fatiha grammatically states exclusivity.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-9:108\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-9:112\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"ٱلْحَٰمِدُونَ names praising servants among the believer’s qualities, complementing ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ: Allah is the object of praise in the Fatiha, while humans are described here as those who praise.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ\"},{\"ref\":\"9:112\",\"ar\":\"ٱلتَّٰٓئِبُونَ ٱلْعَٰبِدُونَ ٱلْحَٰمِدُونَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The shared ح م د root places the divine praise clause beside a human group characterized by praise.\",\"limits\":\"The verses use different grammatical forms and do not specify what the believers praise in 9:112.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-10:10\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The exact formula ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ is the final invocation of the people in the Garden; the Fatiha’s opening praise is also heard as the utterance of a completed communal outcome.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"10:9\",\"ar\":\"فِى جَنَّٰتِ ٱلنَّعِيمِ\"},{\"ref\":\"10:10\",\"ar\":\"وَءَاخِرُ دَعْوَىٰهُمْ أَنِ ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The phrase أَخِرُ دَعْوَىٰهُمْ explicitly marks the formula as their final utterance in that setting.\",\"limits\":\"The formula’s position at the end of this passage does not make the Fatiha itself a description of the Garden.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-10:20\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-14:7\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The conditional لَئِن شَكَرْتُمْ in 14:7 is followed by the statement that Allah remains غَنِىٌّ حَمِيدٌ even if all on earth disbelieve; together they distinguish human gratitude from God’s praiseworthiness in 1:2.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ\"},{\"ref\":\"14:7\",\"ar\":\"لَئِن شَكَرْتُمْ لَأَزِيدَنَّكُمْ ۖ وَلَئِن كَفَرْتُمْ إِنَّ عَذَابِى لَشَدِيدٌۭ\"},{\"ref\":\"14:8\",\"ar\":\"إِن تَكْفُرُوٓا۟ أَنتُمْ وَمَن فِى ٱلْأَرْضِ جَمِيعًۭا فَإِنَّ ٱللَّهَ لَغَنِىٌّ حَمِيدٌ\"}],\"activation\":\"The immediate continuation pairs conditional human response with an attribute from the ح م د root that does not depend on that response.\",\"limits\":\"شُكْر and حَمْد are distinct words; the sequence does not make them interchangeable.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-14:8\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Moses says that even if everyone on earth disbelieves, Allah is still غَنِىٌّ حَمِيدٌ; this makes the praise due to Allah in 1:2 independent of universal human assent.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ\"},{\"ref\":\"14:8\",\"ar\":\"إِن تَكْفُرُوٓا۟ أَنتُمْ وَمَن فِى ٱلْأَرْضِ جَمِيعًۭا فَإِنَّ ٱللَّهَ لَغَنِىٌّ حَمِيدٌ\"}],\"activation\":\"The hypothetical includes all who are on earth, and the divine attribute حَمِيدٌ follows it directly.\",\"limits\":\"حَمِيدٌ describes God as praiseworthy; it is not the same form as the noun ٱلْحَمْدُ.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-14:39\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Ibrahim’s ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ is attached to receiving Ismail and Ishaq in old age, then linked to his Lord hearing supplication; this supplies one specific occasion for the broader praise of 1:2.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ\"},{\"ref\":\"14:39\",\"ar\":\"ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ ٱلَّذِى وَهَبَ لِى عَلَى ٱلْكِبَرِ إِسْمَٰعِيلَ وَإِسْحَٰقَ\"},{\"ref\":\"14:39\",\"ar\":\"إِنَّ رَبِّى لَسَمِيعُ ٱلدُّعَآءِ\"}],\"activation\":\"The praise is followed by a named gift and by the description of Ibrahim’s Lord as hearing prayer.\",\"limits\":\"The Fatiha does not specify this gift or reduce praise to gratitude for one answered prayer.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-15:87\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"سَبْعًۭا مِّنَ ٱلْمَثَانِى may point to the Fatiha, which contains the focus ayah; if so, 1:2 belongs to a passage identified as repeatedly recited.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"15:87\",\"ar\":\"وَلَقَدْ ءَاتَيْنَٰكَ سَبْعًۭا مِّنَ ٱلْمَثَانِى\"}],\"missing\":\"The verse does not name the seven or identify them with the Fatiha; that identification and whether it includes this ayah require further Quranic context or lexical/exegetical evidence.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-15:98\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"فَسَبِّحْ بِحَمْدِ رَبِّكَ pairs glorification with the praise of one’s Lord, echoing the Fatiha’s close grouping of ٱلْحَمْدُ and رَبِّ; praise here is also an act of remembrance amid distress.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"15:97\",\"ar\":\"يَضِيقُ صَدْرُكَ بِمَا يَقُولُونَ\"},{\"ref\":\"15:98\",\"ar\":\"فَسَبِّحْ بِحَمْدِ رَبِّكَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The adjacent hamd and Rabb terms recur together in an imperative response to the Prophet’s distress.\",\"limits\":\"The imperative in 15:98 does not turn the nominal clause in 1:2 into a command.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-16:19\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-16:75\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"After asking whether the incapable slave and the provisioned spender are equal, the verse says ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ; here praise follows a contrast in capacity and giving, though the focus ayah names no such contrast.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"16:75\",\"ar\":\"هَلْ يَسْتَوُۥنَ ۚ ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ ۚ بَلْ أَكْثَرُهُمْ لَا يَعْلَمُونَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The praise clause comes immediately after the rhetorical question contrasting the two figures.\",\"limits\":\"This occurrence does not repeat رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ or explain what specific aspect of the analogy prompts praise.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-17:79\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"مَقَامًۭا مَّحْمُودًۭا is a praised station described as something the Lord may grant the Prophet; the shared ح م د root complements 1:2’s universal praise of Allah with a human station characterized as praiseworthy.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ\"},{\"ref\":\"17:79\",\"ar\":\"عَسَىٰٓ أَن يَبْعَثَكَ رَبُّكَ مَقَامًۭا مَّحْمُودًۭا\"}],\"activation\":\"The noun ٱلْحَمْدُ and adjective مَّحْمُودًا share the ح م د root, while رَبُّكَ is named as the one who raises the Prophet.\",\"limits\":\"The verse does not state who praises this station or equate it with the praise due to Allah.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}"}}
